# Findings gitleaks should not report, one fingerprint per line.
#
# A fingerprint is commit:path:rule:line — it exempts exactly one finding in
# exactly one commit. It does not disable a rule, relax it for a file, or affect
# any other commit, which is why this file is preferred over a `.gitleaks.toml`
# allowlist: nothing here can silently widen later.
#
# NEVER add a fingerprint for a real credential. A committed secret is
# compromised the moment it is pushed — rotate it and follow
# `docs/secrets.md` ("If you commit a secret"). This file is only for values
# that were never secret in the first place.

# A test fixture standing in for a Supabase invite token, in the commit that
# introduced it. It was never a credential — it is a hardcoded string in
# invite.test.ts, matched against itself to assert the emailed link is built from
# whatever generateLink returned.
#
# It tripped generic-api-key because it imitated a real token: a short prefix and
# a random-looking tail, which is exactly the shape the rule looks for. The
# fixture was rewritten to something obviously fake, but the original is still in
# this commit's diff and history is not being rewritten over a false positive.
